## Peak memory (same read, 1M rows, setup included on both sides)

Command (one process per image, identical script, `ru_maxrss` after a warm read
and one timed read):

    flock /tmp/fpq-bench.lock -c 'docker run --rm --shm-size=3g -e BENCH_GIT_REV=<rev> fpq-<image> python3 -c "
    import sys, resource
    sys.path.insert(0, \"/src/bench/rivals/adbc-arrow\"); sys.path.insert(0, \"/src/bench\")
    import sqlalchemy_bench as b
    from lib.dataset import setup
    setup(1000000)
    conn = b.open_dbapi()
    b.read_dbapi(conn)
    ms, n = b.read_dbapi(conn)
    print(n, ms, resource.getrusage(resource.RUSAGE_SELF).ru_maxrss)
    conn.close()"'

baseline  3.83M rows/s  260.9 ms  peak RSS 454.1 MB
change    7.82M rows/s  127.9 ms  peak RSS 198.5 MB

## What a consumer that does ask for tuples pays

On the change image, 1M rows, one process:

    default read (execute + fetchall + len)      127.9 ms   7.82M rows/s
    ResultSet.to_tuples() (C decode of copy)     215.2 ms   4.65M rows/s
    default read then per-row list(result)       292.8 ms   3.42M rows/s
    force_tuples=True (old eager path)           261.3 ms   3.83M rows/s

`to_tuples()` materialises the copy faster than the old row-frame decode; the
per-row Python iteration is the price of laziness for a consumer that reads every
row one at a time, and `force_tuples=True` exists for that consumer.

### AB: 100K DBAPI 3.76M -> 7.23M rows/s (+92%), core 3.55M -> 7.10M (+100%), 4 processes 8.79M -> 17.45M (+99%), stream() 8.76M -> 8.53M (-3%, unchanged path); 1M DBAPI 4.07M -> 8.24M (+102%), core 3.96M -> 8.23M (+108%), 4 processes 9.63M -> 21.61M (+124%), stream() 8.81M -> 8.75M (-1%, unchanged path), peak RSS 454.1 MB -> 198.5 MB (-56%)
